namespace Yello.Infrastructure.Persistence;

/// <summary>
/// One key of the Host's Data Protection key ring, as the framework serialised it. Story 1.10 AC12;
/// Finding F1, open question 7.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para>
/// <b>Why the key ring lives in the database.</b> A Session is an encrypted cookie ticket with no row
/// of its own, and the anti-forgery tokens are encrypted with the same keys. A container gets an empty
/// file system on every start, so a key ring kept there is regenerated by every new revision and every
/// cold start, and every Session and open tab's anti-forgery token is void after it. Keeping the ring
/// beside the data means the next process reads the keys the previous one wrote.
/// </para>
/// <para>
/// <b>Not a domain entity and not Space-scoped.</b> It holds no Space's data, so it carries no
/// <c>SpaceId</c>, no query filter and no row-level security predicate. The key id is generated by the
/// application, as every key here is.
/// </para>
/// <para>
/// <b>Not encrypted at rest.</b> NFR-6 requires encryption in transit only; encryption at rest is one
/// of the five prerequisites recorded under PRD section 6.4's gate in <c>docs/operations.md</c>.
/// </para>
/// </remarks>
internal sealed class DataProtectionKey
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The row's identity, generated by the application.
    /// </summary>
    internal Guid Id { get; init; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The name the framework gave the key when it stored it (for example <c>key-{guid}</c>).
    /// </summary>
    internal string? FriendlyName { get; init; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The key's XML element, exactly as the framework wrote it.
    /// </summary>
    internal required string Xml { get; init; }
}
